12-hour time differences are te worst, especially when you are over 50. I travel regularly between Asia and N America at the moment, and I have completely changed my jet lag strategy. I used to enjoy the flights with nice meals and good wines, but I now completely stopped eating food and drinking alcohol on these 20-25 hour flights. I try to work out and eat in the lounge before the flight, and then I just drink water and tea on the plane. and ask to get my bed made right after take off (for night flights, I prefer those over daytime flights). Then I listen to podcasts or audio books and try to sleep with the help of melatonin. After waking up I work a few hours or read, but I still refuse to eat, except maybe a light meal before arriving. 

Upon arrival also melatonin and/or a sleeping pill an hour before the new sleeping time, and in case I wake up at 4 am or so, stay in bed (and maybe listen to a podcast) instead of getting up and starting to do work. In the morning get up even if you are tired, sit outside in the sun for 20 minutes with a coffee, and then do an easy work out (cardio and resistance training). Last night I slept 7.5 hours after arriving back in Asia from the US East Coast, and today I didn't even take a nap in the afternoon. Flights may be  more boring, but you compensate that in the days after you arrive.

